tools/upload-github.sh always restarted the publication from the beginning: it regenerated the release metadata (bumping its release date and creating a duplicate commit), then failed on `git tag` because the tag already existed, and never reached the uploads. A release whose asset upload failed part way through could therefore not be completed by re-running the script, which is what godotengine/godot-builds#18 asks for.

Release metadata, tags, GitHub releases and assets are now resolved before they are acted upon:

- metadata is generated only when missing, and verified against the release being published (name and git reference) when it already exists;
- an existing tag is verified by checking that the commit it points at contains the release metadata of this release. It is never moved, never force-pushed, and a local/remote disagreement is a hard error;
- an existing GitHub release is reused only when it is attached to the expected tag, and a release without its tag is refused instead of being guessed around;
- assets which are already part of the release are skipped when their size matches, and an unexpected size is an error rather than a silent overwrite, so only the missing assets are uploaded.

GitHub API failures are distinguished from a missing release by probing the repository first and by only treating HTTP 404 as "does not exist", so a network or permission problem is never mistaken for a release to create.

Glob results are now quoted (so filenames with spaces are handled) and `nullglob` is enabled (so a pattern matching nothing is reported instead of being passed to `gh` literally).

Adds tools/tests/, a small shell test harness which runs the real script against a throwaway repository and a stub `gh` executable. It covers the first run, resuming after a failed upload, re-running a complete release, wrong tag targets, tag/release inconsistencies, API failures, partial assets, checksum handling, pre-release/stable flavours and drafts.

