The current example in the documentation:
 ```javascript
 data.map(it => Object.values(it).join(',')).join('\n')
 ```
assumes every object in `data` has the same keys in the same order. This breaks for PsychoPy/PsychoJS experiments, where different routines (trials) can produce objects with different keys (e.g., one routine logs `key_resp.rt`, another logs `inst_resp.rt`), and `Object.values()` misaligns columns across rows instead of throwing an error.

Adding a small `convertToCSV()` utility function to `jatos.js` would help and it will work correctly for both simple and complex (mismatched-key) JSON exports.

This is the function that can be used instead:
```js
function convertToCSV(dataArray, csvSeparator = ",") {

	if (dataArray.length === 0) return '';
	
	// Collect all unique keys across every row
	const headersSet = new Set();
	dataArray.forEach(row => Object.keys(row).forEach(key => headersSet.add(key)));
	const headers = Array.from(headersSet);
	
	const escape = (val) => {
	  val = (val === undefined || val === null) ? '' : String(val);
	  if (val.includes(csvSeparator) || val.includes('"') || val.includes('\n')) {
	    return `"${val.replace(/"/g, '""')}"`;
	  }
	  return val;
	};
	
	const csvRows = [];
	csvRows.push(headers.map(escape).join(csvSeparator));
	
	for (const row of dataArray) {
	  csvRows.push(headers.map(header => escape(row[header])).join(csvSeparator));
	}
	
	return csvRows.join('\n');
